<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day. – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a simple report based on a chart. Interactive: Exchange information about places.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a simple report based on a chart using clear statements (e.g., "This chart shows how many students are recycling in this class."). Can exchange information about local places (e.g., "In my community, there is a recycling center."). Can ask questions about shared information (e.g., "How many people in this school recycle at home?").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of different recyclable items (plastic bottles, paper, aluminum cans, food scraps). Students call out the name of the item. Teacher asks questions like: "What is this?" "Can we recycle it?" "Is it easy to recycle plastic bottles?" Students answer chorally and individually. This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme "We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day." and the scenario "Recycling for Our World."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target sentences and vocabulary for the lesson, focusing on present simple, modal verbs, and "there is/are". Example sentences: "We recycle plastic bottles every day." "There is a recycling bin for plastic." "We can save the environment." The teacher clearly pronounces /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "sustainability" while modeling, exaggerating the sounds for clarity. The teacher also demonstrates segmenting of "environment" by clapping out syllables: en-vi-ron-ment.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Do we recycle plastic bottles at home?" (Expected answer: "Yes, we do" or "No, we don't.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Is 'easy' an adjective or a verb?" (Expected answer: "An adjective.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 3: "What sound is at the beginning of 'recycle'?" (Expected answer: "/r/ sound.")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple bar chart or pictograph titled "What We Recycle in Our School." The chart categorizes items like "Plastic Bottles," "Paper," "Aluminum Cans," and "Food Scraps" with simple numerical values (e.g., 20 plastic bottles, 15 pieces of paper). Below the chart, there is a short, simple dialogue between two students (e.g., 'Ana' and 'Leo') discussing the chart and their recycling habits, incorporating "there is/are," present simple, and target vocabulary. (e.g., Ana: "This chart shows what we recycle. There are 20 plastic bottles." Leo: "Wow! We recycle many bottles. There is a recycling bin for them. What about paper?").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret charts and simple dialogues related to daily routines and environmental actions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (oral):</b> "What does this chart show?" "Who are Ana and Leo talking about?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (oral):</b> "The chart shows we recycle only paper." (False) "Ana says 'There are 20 plastic bottles'." (True)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (oral):</b> "Match the item to the quantity: plastic bottles - 20."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice giving simple reports based on charts, exchanging information about places, and asking questions about shared information using present simple, modal verbs (can/could), there is/are, and target vocabulary (e.g., plastic, bottles, recycling center, save, protect, often, what, where).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition:</b> The teacher displays sentences with blanks related to the chart from Stage 2. Students complete them orally, focusing on target grammar. E.g., "This chart ____ (show) that we ____ (recycle) many plastic bottles." "There ____ (be) a recycling bin for paper." Students repeat the full sentences chorally and individually. The teacher provides immediate feedback on pronunciation, especially /r/ and /s/.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames and vocabulary lists. One student says a sentence, the other substitutes a word from the list. E.g.,
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "We can recycle plastic bottles."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (substitutes 'paper') "We can recycle paper."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "There is a recycling center in my community."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (substitutes 'school') "There is a recycling center in my school."</li>
              </ul>
              This activity also encourages segmenting of multisyllabic words like "community" and "environment."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction – Q&A based on a simple chart:</b> The teacher projects a new, very simple chart showing "Recycling at Home" (e.g., 3 columns: What I recycle, How often, Why). Pairs ask and answer questions about it using guided prompts:
              <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What do you recycle at home?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I recycle plastic bottles and paper."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "How often do you recycle plastic bottles?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I often recycle them."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Why do you recycle?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "We recycle to help the planet and protect the environment."</li>
              </ul>
              The teacher monitors and provides support for language accuracy.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner "Recycling Report Exchange"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs (A and B). Each student receives a unique, simple pictograph or bar chart showing recycling data for a hypothetical "Community A" or "Community B" (e.g., one shows plastic and paper recycling, the other shows glass and compost). The charts use the learned vocabulary (plastic, bottles, paper, compost, recycling center, waste, environment).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A describes their chart to Student B, giving a simple report. Example: "My chart shows recycling in Community A. They recycle 30 plastic bottles. There is a recycling program for paper. They don't compost food scraps often."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens and asks questions to get more information about Community A: "What can they recycle?", "How many plastic bottles do they recycle?", "Is there a recycling center in Community A?", "Could you clarify that?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, Student B describes their chart for Community B to Student A.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A listens and asks questions about Community B.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task directly addresses the specific objective "Give a simple report based on a chart" and "Exchange information about places." Students focus on fluency and collaborative interaction to complete the information exchange.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Recycling Report Exchange" activity.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Recycling Report Exchange" in pairs, giving a simple report and exchanging information.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target words (e.g., recycle, sustainability, environment) clearly.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 5-7 words from the lesson's vocabulary (e.g., plastic, bottles, recycling, bin, save, environment, recycling center).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Reports are simple, clear, and easy to understand.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Students ask and answer questions about the charts and places.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Students attempt to use present simple and "there is/are" correctly.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can ________ about recycling." (e.g., "Today I can talk about recycling.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is __________."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to talk about today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to say in English?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., The pair work with different charts effectively promoted information exchange.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with asking "How often." I need to provide more structured practice for question formation. I could also prepare more visual cues for multisyllabic words.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on the information shared today, where students will write short reports about the recycling habits in Community A or B based on the information they exchanged.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>